.TH TOUCH 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
touch \- change file timestamps, or create empty files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B touch
.RB [ \-acfhm ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR time ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR reference ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR time ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B touch
sets the access time and the modification time of each
.I file
to the current time, or to a time you give.
A
.I file
that does not exist is created empty, unless
.B \-c
is given.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR touch ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The operands are processed in order.
A
.I file
that cannot be touched or created is reported on standard error as
\fBtouch:\fR \fIfile\fB:\fR \fIreason\fR,
where
.I reason
is the system's message, such as
.BR "No such file or directory" ,
and the remaining operands are still processed.
The exit status is then 1.
Nothing is written to standard output.
With no operand, or with an invalid option, it prints a usage message on
standard error and exits with status 1.
.PP
Only the access time and the modification time are set.
The creation time that Windows keeps is never changed.
A file that
.B touch
creates has the current time for whichever of the two times
.B \-a
or
.B \-m
leaves unset, so
.B "touch \-a \-d 2020\-01\-01 new"
gives
.I new
an access time on 2020\-01\-01 and a modification time of the moment it
was created.
.SS Choosing the time
Without
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-r ,
or
.BR \-t ,
the time is the current time.
With
.BR \-r ,
the times are those of the
.I reference
file, to the full resolution that Windows keeps, 100 nanoseconds, with the
access and modification times kept apart.
With
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t ,
both times are the given time, in whole seconds.
If
.B \-d
or
.B \-t
is given more than once, the last one is used.
.B \-d
takes precedence over
.BR \-r ,
but the
.I reference
file must still exist.
.B \-r
and
.B \-t
together are a usage error.
.SS Time formats
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
take the same forms.
The argument must match one form entirely.
Month names are in English.
In the forms with separators, a field may be written with one or two
digits, except the year, which has four, and a space may be any number of
spaces, or none.
A time that matches no form is reported as
\fBtouch: invalid date \(aq\fItime\fB\(aq\fR.
.PP
In the forms with separators, a field that the argument does not give is
taken from the current local time.
That includes the time of day: a date with no time gets the hour, minute,
and second of the moment
.B touch
runs, not midnight, and a time of day with no seconds gets the current
second.
The digits-only form at the end of the list takes omitted fields on its
left from the current time, and omitted seconds as 0.
.TP
\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A time of day on the current date.
.TP
\fIMM\fB.\fIDD\fB\-\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A month, day, and time in the current year.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB.\fIMM\fB.\fIDD\fB\-\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A full date and time.
.TP
\fIMon\fR \fIDD\fR \fIhh\fB:\fImm\fB:\fIss\fR \fIYYYY\fR
.I Mon
is a month name in full or abbreviated to 3 letters, in any case.
There is no weekday.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB\-\fIMM\fB\-\fIDD\fR[ \fIhh\fR[\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]]]
A date, with a time of day, an hour, or neither.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB\-\fIMM\fB\-\fIDD\fR \fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR] \fIzone\fR
A date and time in another time zone, converted to the local one.
.I zone
is
.BI + hhmm
or
.BI \- hhmm
for an offset from UTC of up to 12 hours,
.BI + hh
or
.BI \- hh
for whole hours, or
.B Z
for UTC.
The offset is truncated to a multiple of 36 seconds, so minutes that are
not a multiple of 3 are inexact.
.TP
\fB@\fIseconds\fR
Seconds since 1970\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC, as decimal digits with an
optional minus sign and nothing else.
.TP
[[[[[\fICC\fR]\fIYY\fR]\fIMM\fR]\fIDD\fR]\fIhh\fR]\fImm\fR[\fB.\fIss\fR]
Digits only, in one of these forms:
.IR mm ,
.IR hhmm ,
.IR DDhhmm ,
.IR MMDDhhmm ,
.IR YYMMDDhhmm ,
or
.IR CCYYMMDDhhmm ,
followed by an optional
.BI . ss\fR.
.I YY
is the year within 50 years of the current year.
.PP
A month of 0 or 13, a day of 0 or 32, an hour of 24, and a minute of 60
are errors, except that in the digits-only form a month or day of 00 is
carried back into the previous year or month.
A seconds field of 60 is carried into the next minute, and so is 61 in
the forms with separators, but 61 is an error in the digits-only form.
A day that the month lacks, such as February 30, is carried into the next
month.
.PP
A time without a zone is read in the local time zone, which is that of
.BR TZ ,
or else the Windows time zone setting, and whether daylight saving time
applies is decided from the date.
The result must lie from 1970\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC through 3001\-01\-01
07:59:59 UTC, or through 3000\-12\-31 23:59:59 UTC for a time that ends
in a zone.
A time outside that range, in any form but
.BR @ ,
is an invalid date.
An
.BI @ seconds
outside it, including every negative value, is not an error but is taken
to be the epoch, 1970\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-am ,
and may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-t202601011200 ,
or be the next argument.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes its
argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-no\-create
Do not create files.
A file that does not exist is skipped, with no message and no effect on
the exit status.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-dereference
Touch a symbolic link itself instead of the file it points to.
A file that does not exist is still created.
It does not affect
.BR \-r ,
which always uses the file a link points to.
.TP
.B \-a
Set only the access time, and leave the modification time unchanged.
With
.B \-m
as well, both are set, as with neither.
.TP
.B \-m
Set only the modification time, and leave the access time unchanged.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fItime\fR, \fB\-\-date\fR \fItime\fR
Use
.I time
instead of the current time; see
.BR "Time formats" .
It takes precedence over
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI "\-t " time
The same as
.BR \-d .
There is no long form.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR \fIreference\fR, \fB\-\-reference\fR \fIreference\fR
Use the access and modification times of
.I reference
instead of the current time.
If
.I reference
cannot be examined,
.B touch
reports it and exits with status 1 before touching any file.
Incompatible with
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-f
Accepted and ignored.
The usage message omits it.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal, applied when
.B touch
starts.
If it has the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
a file that
.B touch
creates is read-only.
Its other bits have no visible effect, and existing files are not
changed.
In the kit's shell,
.B touch
uses the shell's mask instead; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.TP
.B TZ
The local time zone, in the format of the Microsoft C runtime, such as
.B UTC0
or
.BR EST5EDT ;
see
.BR date (1).
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
read times in it, except
.BI @ seconds
and times that end in a zone.
When it is unset or empty, the Windows time zone setting applies.
A value in another form, such as
.BR Europe/Paris ,
is misread, not ignored.
In the kit's shell a change may have no effect; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and Windows error dialogs on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a missing file with
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-\-help
as the first argument.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, no operand,
.B \-r
with
.BR \-t ,
an invalid date, a
.B \-r
file that cannot be examined, or any
.I file
that could not be touched or created.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an empty file, or set the times of a file to now:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Update a file only if it exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-c build.stamp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set both times of a file, in local time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-d \(aq2026\-10\-19 12:00:00\(aq report.txt
$ touch \-t 202610191200.30 report.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set the times to a time given in UTC:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-d \(aq2026\-10\-19 12:00:00 Z\(aq report.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a file the times of another file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-r main.c main.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a file older than everything else, so that
.BR make (1)
rebuilds it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-d @0 main.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set only the access time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch \-a log.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR touch :
.IP \(bu 2
A time is not free-form text.
Words such as
.B now
and
.BR yesterday ,
relative times, weekday names, a
.B T
between date and time, fractional seconds, time zone names, and
.B AM
or
.B PM
are errors.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B \-d
date with no time of day gets the current time of day, not midnight.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
also takes the forms of
.BR \-d ,
and
.B \-d
takes the digits-only forms of
.BR \-t .
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B \-
operand is a file name, not standard output.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B \-\-time
or
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-help
works only as the first argument.
.PP
Windows keeps a creation time, a last access time, and a last write time
for each file.
.B touch
sets the last two.
It works on read-only files and on directories.
.I /dev/null
is accepted and left alone, but a Windows device name such as
.B nul
cannot be touched; the error is
.BR "Function not implemented" .
.PP
Paths may have a drive letter, as in
.IR c:/dir/file ,
and backslashes, but prefer forward slashes, since in the shell a
backslash quotes the next character.
Names are not case sensitive on most volumes, so
.B touch Foo
updates an existing
.I foo
instead of creating
.IR Foo .
A name that Windows does not allow, such as one containing
.BR ? ,
fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B touch
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B touch
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
There
.B touch
uses the shell's file mode creation mask, which the shell's
.B umask
command sets, and ignores
.B BB_UMASK
given on its command line.
The C runtime reads
.B TZ
once per process, at the first time conversion, so once the shell has
converted a time, as for an earlier
.BR "touch \-d" ,
a change to
.BR TZ ,
even on the command line, does not affect later
.B touch
commands in that shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR stat (1)
